{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/i107q5of","slideIndex":4,"text":"Primary question: how does target-prevalence decline affect retrieval across models and pathologies? Secondary question: from the same SwAV initialization, how does supervised contrastive adaptation compare with classification? The study is an evaluation protocol, not a newly proposed backbone architecture.\n\nSource: supplied Chest_XRay_Retrieval_IEEE_Format.docx. Reference numbers follow the manuscript."}
{"kind":"slide","id":"sl/x8f69ofe","slideIndex":5,"layout":{"id":"ly/elwrupwj","name":"ICAST Two Column"},"title":"Proposed Methodology","textShapes":5,"backgroundColor":"rgba(250,250,250,1)"}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/yhg7epsj","slideIndex":5,"position":{"left":120,"top":0,"width":1040,"height":104.57},"name":"Text 1","placeholder":"title","text":"Proposed Methodology","geometry":"rect","style":{"fontSize":48,"typeface":"Roboto","color":"#FFFFFF","alignment":"center"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/zi98nu94","slideIndex":5,"position":{"left":57.6,"top":148.8,"width":566.4,"height":480},"name":"Text 1","placeholder":"body","text":"Data: NIH, SIIM-ACR and three external chest X-ray collections\nModels: six pretrained or adapted ResNet-50 conditions\nProtocol: 200 queries per target, 20,000 images per gallery\nPrevalence: 5%, 2%, 1% and 0.5%, with five gallery samples each","geometry":"rect","style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Roboto","color":"#333333","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/87ipkzal","slideIndex":5,"position":{"left":662.4,"top":595.2,"width":559.68,"height":38.4},"name":"Text 2","placeholder":"body","text":"Embedding extraction and exact retrieval workflow","geometry":"rect","style":{"fontSize":16,"typeface":"Roboto","color":"#6B7280","italic":true,"alignment":"center"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/ml07i9sv","slideIndex":5,"position":{"left":662.4,"top":148.8,"width":559.68,"height":441.6},"name":"Text 2","text":"1  224 × 224 radiograph\n\n2  ResNet-50 encoder\n\n3  2,048-D normalized embedding\n\n4  Exact cosine ranking","geometry":"rect","style":{"fontSize":26,"typeface":"Roboto","color":"#0B2545","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/2p0n2lg7","slideIndex":5,"position":{"left":1173.52,"top":23.04,"width":58.48,"height":58.48},"name":"Slide Number","placeholder":"slideNumber","text":"6","geometry":"ellipse","fillColor":"#FFFFFF","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Roboto","color":"#0B2545","bold":true,"alignment":"center"}}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/x8f69ofe","slideIndex":5,"text":"Three evaluated targets: cardiomegaly, emphysema and pneumothorax. Relevant target counts are 1,000, 400, 200 or 100 per 20,000-image gallery. Three non-target pathology distractors contribute 400 images each. Background fills the remaining gallery positions. There are 60 galleries in total. NIH target-positive patients are excluded from adaptation/background partitions. NIH adaptation uses 48,280 training and 12,266 validation images. Common-class adaptation uses 4,545 source-class-balanced images split 80:20. Query patients are excluded where identifiers permit. TB was excluded as an evaluated target after a source-bias audit, but remains a fixed 2% distractor. The pipeline describes an exact exhaustive search using normalized embeddings.\n\nSource: supplied Chest_XRay_Retrieval_IEEE_Format.docx. Reference numbers follow the manuscript."}
{"kind":"slide","id":"sl/gnmp4jqx","slideIndex":6,"layout":{"id":"ly/14ra9krq","name":"ICAST Title Only"},"title":"Experimental Setup","textShapes":2,"backgroundColor":"rgba(250,250,250,1)"}
